==English==

===Etymology===
The verb 'To Waz/Wazz' in northern English dialect means to urinate. In both West Yorkshire and East Lancashire, offal was very popular and when produced en masse the offal was cooked in linen socks that prevented the offal from being burnt and ruined when it came in to direct contact with the vessel in which it was being cooked.  Unsurprisingly these linens absorbed the odour of the offal (which is similar to urine) and were discarded. They were colloquially referred to as Waz Socks. The derogatory sense of a wazzock to this day refers to someone discarded of having any purpose or worth.

Possibly from {{term|wiseacre}}.

